latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Identify all 'E' shapes in the grid.\n   b. For each 'E', determine the current state of its middle and bottom bars (full or partial).\n   c. Create a data structure to represent the grid of 'E's, storing their positions and current states.\n\n2. Determine the Ideal Pattern:\n   a. Count the total number of 'E's in the grid.\n   b. Calculate the ideal number of 'E's with full middle bars and partial bottom bars (Type A), and those with partial middle bars and full bottom bars (Type B).\n   c. The goal is to have roughly equal numbers of Type A and Type B, with a slight preference for Type A if the total is odd.\n\n3. Identify Discrepancies:\n   a. Compare the current state of each 'E' to the ideal pattern.\n   b. Mark 'E's that need to be changed to achieve the ideal pattern.\n\n4. Apply Symmetry Rules:\n   a. Start from the corners and edges of the grid, working inwards.\n   b. Ensure that opposite corners have matching 'E' types.\n   c. Create a symmetrical pattern along both horizontal and vertical axes.\n\n5. Resolve Conflicts:\n   a. If applying symmetry rules conflicts with the ideal count of Type A and Type B 'E's, prioritize symmetry.\n   b. Make minimal adjustments to maintain both symmetry and balance as much as possible.\n\n6. Update the Grid:\n   a. Based on the decisions made in steps 4 and 5, update the 'E' shapes in the grid.\n   b. Ensure that all top bars remain full and vertical segments remain unchanged.\n   c. Modify only the middle and bottom bars of the 'E's as determined.\n\n7. Final Check:\n   a. Verify that the resulting grid maintains the correct structure for all 'E's.\n   b. Confirm that the pattern is symmetrical and balanced.\n   c. Ensure no black separating lines were altered.\n\n8. Return the Transformed Grid:\n   a. Convert the internal representation back to a ColoredGrid object.\n   b. Return the final, transformed grid.\n\nImplementation Considerations:\n- Use helper functions for each major step to keep the code modular and readable.\n- Implement a function to check if two 'E's are symmetrical (considering their position in the grid).\n- Use a scoring system to evaluate different possible arrangements, helping to choose the most balanced and symmetrical solution.\n- Consider implementing a backtracking algorithm if simple greedy approaches don't yield satisfactory results.\n\nThis plan allows for a more flexible and context-aware solution that can handle the variations seen in the test cases while still maintaining the overall structure and symmetry of the 'E' patterns in the grid."
